==Portuguese==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|pt|roa-opt|aleiman}}, from {{inh|pt|la|Alamannus}}, from {{der|pt|gem-pro|*Alamann-}}. {{doublet|pt|alamano}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA|br=alemão|pt=alemão}}
* {{hyph|pt|a|le|mão}}

===Adjective===
{{pt-adj|f=alemã|mpl=alemães|hascomp=no}}

# [[German]] {{gloss|of or relating to Germany}}
# [[Germanic]] {{gloss|of, belonging or relating to the German language}}
# [[Germanic]] {{gloss|relating or belonging the Germanic ethnicity}}
#: {{syn|pt|germano|germânico}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|slang}} [[blond]] and fair-skinned

====See also====
* {{l|pt|abarrosado}}
* {{l|pt|alemoado}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|alemães|f=alemã}}

# [[German]] {{gloss|person from Germany}}
# [[German]] {{gloss|person of German ancestry}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|slang}} a person with [[blond]] hair and [[fair]] skin
#: {{syn|pt|galego|q1=Northeast Brazil, Florianópolis, slang|polaco|q2=Paraná, slang}}
# {{lb|pt|chiefly [[criminal]] [[slang]]}} [[cop]] {{gloss|police officer}}
#: {{syn|pt|coxinha|q1=slang}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|-}}

# [[German]] {{gloss|Germanic language spoken mostly in Central Europe}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|person of German ancestry}} in Brazil, this typically includes descendants of Germanic immigrants from the former Austro-Hungarian Empire
* {{sense|German language}} in South Brazil, this may mean {{l|en|Hunsrik}} in reference to local speakers; in other circumstances, it typically refers to {{l|en|High German}}
* {{m|pt|alemoa}} is an informal feminine used by some speakers in Brazil

====Related terms====
* {{l|pt|Alemanha}}

